What you're building
A code generator that turns a written spec into working client code via an agentic coding tool. The generator is the deliverable. The messaging app below is just the example we'll use to evaluate it.

The generator can take whatever shape suits you — CLI, slash commands, prompts, sub-agents, etc. We care about reproducibility, not form.

Core promise: if we delete the generated client code and run your generator, we get back working clients whose behavior matches the spec. We may verify by regenerating.

The example project
A minimal messaging app: a small server and two clients in different languages. Your choice of languages — anything that can't share source (Swift + Kotlin, Python + Swift, i.e. no shared ReactNative for all clients). We want to see that you can think through the spec generic vs platform specific extensions.

Target environment
Local only; emulator or localhost is fine. No 3rd party external services. We should be able to duplicate your results it in a sandbox.

Required behavior:

A user identifies themselves by name on first launch (i.e. login).
A user sends a text message addressed to another user.
Messages queue locally when offline and flush when the client reconnects.
Client displays messages received from the server.
The server may be trivial — single process, in-memory, no security, no on disk persistence, DDoS prevention, etc. Don't over invest there - main goal is messaging protocol design and something to host it.

Your spec must be precise enough that both clients behave identically in scenarios like:

Alice → Bob: "Hi Bob, I have something important to tell you"
Bob → Alice: "What is it?"
Alice goes offline, queues a message
Bob goes offline, queues a message
Alice reconnects; her queued message reaches the server
Alice goes offline again
Bob reconnects; receives Alice's message from the server and delivers his own queued message
How you demonstrate this works is up to you.

Constraints
Agentic tool: Claude Code or Codex, so we can grade reproducibly.
No turnkey messaging SDKs. Implement the protocol and offline behavior from your spec. General-purpose libraries (HTTP, WebSocket, JSON, storage) are fine; messaging/chat SDKs are not — they hide what we're asking you to specify.
Local only. No cloud, no third-party services.
Deliverables
One or more git repos, delivered as an zip/archive (or archives). Organize them however you like — single repo, separate repos per platform, separate repo for the generator, etc. A README must make it obvious where to find:

The agentic harness — what you did to create a re-usable agentic harness that can drive reliable code generation.
The spec — your source of truth (what you built to create clients driven by harness).
The generator — what to run, in which tool, to regenerate clients.
The generated client code — committed, with the regeneration boundary marked.
The server — write it, but it does not have to be under generator discipline.
Future.md — what you'd do next (optional).
Commit history matters — we read it to see how you spent the time.

How we evaluate
Signal	What we look for
DESIGN.md	Shows your design principles.
CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, etc.	Looking at your setup.
Be clear about what's generated vs. hand-written.	A mix is fine. Mark the boundary in your README — we want to understand what you did vs what you delegated to the coding agent.
Author attribution	Be clear about what's generated vs. hand-crafted. Mark which artifacts you wrote vs. delegated to the agent.
Commit history	showcases your work, thought process, do not check-in single final output.
Regeneration works	We delete the parts you marked as generated, follow your README, get working clients. Primary criterion.
Spec is authoritative	Behavior questions resolved by reading the spec, not the code.
Two clients can work with each other through the server	The spec actually constrains both implementations that behave the same.
Evolution	Assume that spec(s) will be evolving - your harness is generic and not hard-coded to single generation of this exact spec - more features/layers/modules could be added later. I.e. could we write additional rules into spec - attachments, reactions, group conversations, protocol extensions? Would harness still generate working clients given good spec(s) extensions
Workspace shape	The structure you chose matches the problem. No specific layout required; we're looking for judgment.
Quality	agentic coding is tricky, how are you thinking about quality, what did you do to test this is working.
FUTURE.md	We understand this is a time constrained exercise. Optional place to talk about what else you would do.